WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.640
Jason Momoa has been playing DC's Aquaman for&nbsp;
more than 5 years – but with a new creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.740
team behind the DC movie machine, rumors are&nbsp;
swirling that he might be about to double-dip.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.540
With anti-heroes like Venom, Deadpool, and&nbsp;
Black Adam making waves at the box office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:20.700
with their unorthodox methods and off-beat senses&nbsp;
of humor, it's natural to wonder whether some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.900
the other anti-heroes of the comic book world&nbsp;
are going to get feature films of their own.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.340
Now more than ever, a film centered on&nbsp;
a filthy-mouthed bounty hunter like Lobo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.600
seems like a distinct possibility — if&nbsp;
only an actor can be found to embody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.800
the character's hyper-violent nature&nbsp;
and unusual fondness for profanity.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:43.080
Who has the x-factor necessary to bring Lobo&nbsp;
from the page to the screen? One obvious choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.360
thanks to his experience playing the barbaric&nbsp;
Khal Drogo on "Game of Thrones" is Jason Momoa.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:54.480
There's only one problem: Momoa is already busy&nbsp;
in the DC Extended Universe playing Aquaman. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.860
that can't stop fans from dreaming about Momoa&nbsp;
in the role of Lobo, whether or not such an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:03.360
arrangement would ever be practically possible.&nbsp;
And if you take a look at some recently released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.640
fan art depicting Momoa in the role, you have to&nbsp;
admit he'd make a good fit, and it seems evident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.140
that he'd be able to make the Ultimate Bastich as&nbsp;
entertaining on the screen as he is on the page.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.700
Fans are right to speculate on who might&nbsp;
portray Lobo on the big screen. And Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.520
Momoa was recently asked about the possibility in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.660
a ComicBook.com interview to promote his&nbsp;
upcoming "Slumberland" film on Netflix.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:30.120
James Gunn, set to take over the film wing&nbsp;
of DC Comics, posted a picture of Lobo on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.320
his official Mastodon account, leading&nbsp;
some to assume that a Lobo feature film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.160
is in the works. When asked about it,&nbsp;
Momoa didn't shut down the prospect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.200
his duties as Aquaman notwithstanding. Having&nbsp;
recently discussed an upcoming "dream project"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.540
within the DC Extended Universe, Momoa doesn't&nbsp;
deny that such a project could involve Lobo:

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:53.700
"Huh. Well, everyone knows I'm a big comic&nbsp;
book fan and so the comic that I collect the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.300
most and I have every comic there is ... you&nbsp;
can do your research and find out what it is."

00:01:57.561 --> 00:01:59.421
"I love you too."

00:01:59.421 --> 00:02:04.500
Could this be a reference to Lobo? Would&nbsp;
audiences accept two DC superheroes portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.180
by the same actor across multiple projects?&nbsp;
Would Lobo and Aquaman get to fight each other?

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.980
None of these questions are answered by the&nbsp;
excellent piece by fan art maestro Bosslogic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.720
on Instagram depicting Momoa as Lobo, but&nbsp;
it looks pretty cool anyway. The picture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.340
shows Momoa in full Lobo gear and makeup,&nbsp;
and the comments seem mostly enthusiastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:28.560
about the prospect — even with the Atlantis-sized&nbsp;
conflict of interest the casting would represent.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.940
Bosslogic's rendition of Jason Momoa&nbsp;
in the role of Lobo looks good and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.540
true to the original character. But&nbsp;
it also calls to mind another Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.680
Momoa comic book movie project that&nbsp;
never came to fruition: "The Crow."

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.080
"The Crow" was famously first adapted for&nbsp;
a movie in the 1990s with Brandon Lee as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.040
the titular gloomy crimefighter,&nbsp;
and for a time, Momoa was attached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:54.960
to the project. Unfortunately, it didn't&nbsp;
happen, and Momoa even apologized to fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.960
of the character for his inability&nbsp;
to get the project off the ground.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:03.120
Looking at Momoa as Lobo in Bosslogic's fan&nbsp;
art, it's difficult not to see the similarities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.200
between both characters' distinctive&nbsp;
facial features, and lament that neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.240
Momoa's "Lobo" or Momoa's "The Crow" ever made&nbsp;
their way to the screen — at least, not yet.

